      "role": "user",
      "content": "so you're saying the priority is basically set by the existential nature of the threat. but if we follow that logic, isn't it essentially a moral imperative to push through these transitions regardless of the immediate fallout? if a policy makes life significantly harder for working-class families through high heating/fuel costs or causes major job losses in manufacturing, isn't that just an acceptable cost for \"future survival\"? it feels like you're framing the economic argument as a secondary logistical hurdle rather than a legitimate conflict of interests.",
